' ===========================================================================
' NAPSCRIPT.BM - the test hooks of the tactical game. Included at the end of
' NAPOLEON.BAS. SCRIPT.BM holds the same hooks for the strategic game, and its
' scriptbattle sets NAP_SEED for each battle a script plays. Until 2026-11-10
' PDS 7.1 compiled them too, for the bridge, now retired (docs\devnotes.md,
' item 60).
'
' An environment variable chooses how the program runs:
'   NAP_SEED   RANDOMIZE takes this number instead of TIMER, and the program
'              is "scripted" (scripted = 1): each wait for a key is
'              announced, and TICK waits for a key where it read one. A TICK
'              shorter than a second reads no key, in DOS either, so under a
'              script it returns at once. PLAY and SOUND are silent
'              (silence, QB64.BM).
' A program started without it plays as before.
' ===========================================================================

' Chooses the mode and seeds the random numbers. It stands where
' NAPOLEON.BAS had RANDOMIZE TIMER, its first statement.
SUB napstart
seed$ = ENVIRON$("NAP_SEED")
IF seed$ <> "" THEN
	scripted = 1
	CALL silence
END IF
IF seed$ = "" THEN RANDOMIZE TIMER ELSE RANDOMIZE VAL(seed$)
END SUB

' Called in TICK once sec! is positive. A pause of a second or more reads
' keys, so it is announced as a wait at place 3; when the program is
' scripted, it waits for a key and gives -1, and TICK returns. A shorter
' pause reads no key and is not announced; when the program is scripted it
' gives -1, and TICK returns at once. Otherwise it gives 0, and TICK pauses
' as before.
FUNCTION scripttick% (sec!)
scripttick% = 0
IF scripted = 0 THEN EXIT FUNCTION
IF sec! >= 1 THEN CALL announce(3, "")
IF sec! >= 1 THEN DO WHILE INKEY$ = "": LOOP
scripttick% = -1
END FUNCTION

' Called right before each SYSTEM that ends the battle, once OUTCOME.&&& is
' written if it is. It announces a wait at place 7, with the letter that
' says how the battle ended: O by over (time up, or a retreat), W by
' wipeout with time left, Q by F10. When the program is scripted it waits
' for a key, so that a script can take the state of the last screen: the
' PDS 7.1 runtime goes back to text mode at SYSTEM. In normal play it does
' nothing.
SUB scriptend (letter$)
CALL announce(7, letter$)
IF scripted THEN DO WHILE INKEY$ = "": LOOP
END SUB

' Says that the program is about to wait for a key, when it is scripted.
' place names the kind of wait (1 to 255) and letter$ narrows it
' (its first character, or ""). The places are the strategic game's where
' the two games share a kind of wait:
'   1  a pause that any key ends ("hit a key")
'   2  a menu; letter$ is the first letter of the menu's title
'   3  TICK of a second or more
'   5  the command for a unit (order, wait3)
'   6  the cursor on the map: move, intelligence, inspire (order, wait5)
'   7  the end of the battle, before SYSTEM (scriptend)
' Byte &H4F1 holds the place, &H4F2 the letter, and &H4F0 counts the waits:
' QB64Rust's tools\screen\README.md, "Prompts". It leaves DEF SEG at 0, as
' BuffClear does.
SUB announce (place, letter$)
IF scripted = 0 THEN EXIT SUB
code = 0
IF letter$ <> "" THEN code = ASC(UCASE$(letter$))
DEF SEG = 0
POKE &H4F1, place
POKE &H4F2, code
POKE &H4F0, (PEEK(&H4F0) + 1) AND 255
END SUB
